---
// A single take, listed but not yet playable in this task — `/takes/[id]`
// is Task 6's own detail page. This is a domain-specific composite (a Take
// + its instruments + the song/event context it's being listed under)
// rather than a generic primitive, so it lives here rather than in
// packages/ui — see the boundary rule in task-5-report.md.
//
// --- Variant design (Task 6, replacing Task 5's two ad-hoc boolean-ish
// props) -----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `context` is a discriminated union on `kind`, not a pair of optional
// `event`/`song` props a caller could set both-or-neither of by accident:
//
//   - "song"  — listed under a song page (`event` given): the row's own
//     link is the recorded date (this list is already scoped to one song
//     and ordered chronologically), plus a second link to the event.
//   - "event" — listed under an event page (`song` given): the row's own
//     link is the song title (that page's whole point is "what was
//     played"); no second link, since the page itself IS the event.
//   - "full"  — listed somewhere that is scoped to neither a single song
//     nor a single event (Task 6 adds three such places: `/search`, the
//     home page's "needs your vote", and its favorited-takes list): the
//     row needs the song title AND a recorded date AND a link to the
//     event, because none of those is implied by the page it's on.
//
// A caller can only construct one shape per `kind` — no "song and event
// both given", no "neither given", which the old `event?`/`song?` pair
// allowed silently (and which would have rendered a nonsensical row: two
// links reading the same thing, or a date-only row with no way to reach
// what was played).
//
// Two links reading the same text but pointing at different places would
// fail WCAG 2.4.4 (link purpose from text alone) — the second link is
// always phrased as "View this rehearsal/concert/session", never a repeat
// of the take's own label.
//
// --- The seam for increments 4 and 5 --------------------------------
//
// Two named slots, empty today, are the extension points increments 4 and
// 5 live in — NOT a fourth prop each, and not a fork of this component:
//
//   - `leading`  — increment 4's play/pause affordance, rendered before the
//     take's own label. Unfilled today, so nothing renders there (an empty
//     `<slot>` emits no markup at all) — satisfying the brief's "no fake
//     play affordance" rule by construction, not by a conditional the next
//     increment has to remember to remove.
//   - `trailing` — increment 5's vote control (cast/change a keeper vote),
//     rendered in the same trailing column as the duration, so a take row
//     ends with "how long" and "what the room thinks" side by side.
//
// Neither slot needs a new prop, a new fork, or a change to `context` —
// increments 4/5 fill them from the caller (`<TakeRow ...><Fragment
// slot="leading">...</Fragment></TakeRow>`), and every existing call site
// (song/event/full) keeps working unchanged.
//
// `TakeRowContext` itself (and its two ref types) lives in the sibling
// `take-row-context.ts`, not exported from here — see that file's header
// comment for why (an Astro server-build compiler bug, not a style choice).
//
// --- `transitionName` (Task 6 review round 1, F1) --------------------
//
// Every row used to hardcode `transition:name={`take-${take.id}`}`. That's
// fine as long as a given take id is rendered at most once per page — but
// `/` and `/me` both list takes across more than one section (favorites,
// recent events, needs-your-vote / favorites, votes), and a take that's
// both a favorite AND e.g. unvoted lands in two of them at once. Two
// elements sharing one `view-transition-name` isn't a warning — per the
// CSS View Transitions spec it silently aborts the transition for the
// WHOLE page, not just those two elements. `transitionName` lets the
// caller de-duplicate: omit it (or pass `undefined`) for the normal case
// and it defaults to `take-${take.id}`; pass `null` to suppress the
// attribute entirely for a take id the same page render already used once.
//
// --- `favorited` (Task 6 review round 1, F4) --------------------------
//
// Gold's only prior use was a decorative star next to a "Your favorites"
// heading that already said the word "favorites" — no information. This
// prop puts the same star per-row instead, shown only when THIS row's take
// is one of the current member's favorites — real, row-specific
// information on pages that mix favorited and non-favorited takes (`/`'s
// recent-events/needs-your-vote sections, `/search`, `/takes/[id]`'s hero).
//
// --- `data-take-id` (Task 6, Fix round 3) ------------------------------
//
// The link that carries `transition:name` also carries `data-take-id`, a
// plain hook for `TakeTransitionRetarget.astro`'s click-delegated script
// (imported only by the pages that can render the same take twice — see
// that component's own header comment). It has no effect at all without
// that script: with JS off, this attribute just sits there unused and
// round 1's first-occurrence-wins naming is exactly what still happens.
import type { instrumentsRepo, takesRepo } from "@bandlib/db";
import { formatDuration, formatShortDate } from "../server/format.js";
import { TAKE_STATE_LABEL } from "../server/take-state.js";
import type { TakeRowContext } from "./take-row-context.js";

export interface Props {
  take: takesRepo.Take;
  instruments: instrumentsRepo.Instrument[];
  context: TakeRowContext;
  /** `undefined` (the default, when omitted) → `take-${take.id}`. `null` →
   *  no `transition:name` at all. A caller listing the same take id more
   *  than once on one page must pass `null` for every occurrence after the
   *  first — see this file's header comment. */
  transitionName?: string | null;
  /** Shows the gold favorite marker next to the take's label. Defaults to
   *  `false` — see this file's header comment. */
  favorited?: boolean;
}

const { take, instruments, context, transitionName, favorited = false } = Astro.props;
const resolvedTransitionName =
  transitionName === null ? undefined : (transitionName ?? `take-${take.id}`);

const event = context.kind !== "event" ? context.event : undefined;
const song = context.kind !== "song" ? context.song : undefined;

// "song" mode's own list is already chronological — the date IS the thing
// that orients a reader there, never suppressed just because the take also
// has a label. "event" and "full" mode both use the song title instead,
// since "what was played" (not "when") is what those lists are for. A
// label (when present) renders as a sublabel underneath in every mode,
// never as a replacement for the primary link text.
const primaryLabel = song ? song.title : formatShortDate(take.recordedAt);
// "full" mode alone needs an explicit date line — it's the one context
// where the date isn't implied by the page (a song page's rows already
// linger under that song; an event page's rows already share one date).
const showDate = context.kind === "full";
---

<div class="bl-take-row">
  <slot name="leading" />
  <div class="bl-take-row-main">
    <a
      href={`/takes/${take.id}`}
      class="bl-take-label"
      transition:name={resolvedTransitionName}
      data-take-id={take.id}
    >
      {primaryLabel}
    </a>
    {favorited && (
      <span class="bl-favorite-mark bl-take-favorite-mark" title="One of your favorites">
        <svg aria-hidden="true" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="currentColor">
          <path d="M8 0.8l2.1 4.5 4.9.7-3.6 3.5.9 4.9L8 12.2l-4.3 2.2.9-4.9-3.6-3.5 4.9-.7z" />
        </svg>
        <span class="sr-only">, one of your favorites</span>
      </span>
    )}
    {take.label && <span class="bl-take-meta bl-take-sublabel">{take.label}</span>}
    <div class="bl-take-meta bl-take-row-line">
      <span class={`bl-badge ${take.state === "keeper" ? "bl-badge-keeper" : ""}`}>
        {TAKE_STATE_LABEL[take.state]}
      </span>
      {showDate && <span>{formatShortDate(take.recordedAt)}</span>}
      {event && (
        <a href={`/events/${event.id}`} class="bl-take-event-link">
          View this {event.kind}
        </a>
      )}
    </div>
    {instruments.length > 0 && (
      <div class="bl-take-instruments">
        {instruments.map((instrument) => (
          <span class="bl-take-instrument-tag">{instrument.label}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
  <div class="bl-take-row-trailing">
    <span class="bl-take-row-duration">{formatDuration(take.durationMs)}</span>
    <slot name="trailing" />
  </div>
</div>
